# 🧪 Network Troubleshooting Commands

The following commands were used to verify the server configuration.

## 1. Check hostname

```cmd
hostname
```

Purpose:

Displays the computer's hostname.

Expected:

```text
SERVER01
```

---

## 2. Check complete IP configuration

```cmd
ipconfig /all
```

Purpose:

Displays detailed network configuration, including:

* IPv4 address
* Subnet mask
* Default gateway
* DHCP information
* DNS information
* MAC address

---

## 3. Test default gateway

```cmd
ping 10.0.2.2
```

Purpose:

Tests connectivity between SERVER01 and the VirtualBox NAT gateway.

---

## 4. Test external IP connectivity

```cmd
ping 8.8.8.8
```

Purpose:

Tests whether the server can reach an external IP address without relying on DNS name resolution.

---

## 5. Test DNS name resolution and connectivity

```cmd
ping google.com
```

Purpose:

Tests both network connectivity and DNS name resolution.

---

## 6. Test DNS resolution directly

```cmd
nslookup google.com
```

Purpose:

Checks whether DNS can resolve a hostname into an IP address.

---

# 🔎 Troubleshooting Methodology

A useful network troubleshooting sequence practiced today was:

```text
Check IP configuration
        ↓
Check default gateway
        ↓
Test external IP
        ↓
Test domain name
        ↓
Test DNS directly
```

Example:

```text
ipconfig /all
        ↓
ping 10.0.2.2
        ↓
ping 8.8.8.8
        ↓
ping google.com
        ↓
nslookup google.com
```

This approach helps identify whether the problem is related to:

* IP configuration
* Local connectivity
* Gateway
* Internet connectivity
* DNS resolution

